Z||000005||triker||05-23-2000||02:10 PM||||The idea of building a trike out of a Fiero started about 10 years ago, shortly after I bought my GT.  My cousin used to work for a company in LA that built dune trikes out of VW's.  After taking a good look at the Fiero, I knew it would be a fairly easy conversion.  I got serious about it early last summer and found a car with a totaled front end.  I didn't want to destroy a good car.  It took me about 2 months of evenings and weekends to get it running.  I didn't know if it would work or not.  Would the front end stay on the ground? (wheely bars?).  Would it push so bad it wouldn't turn (turning brakes?).  But believe it or not, it handles better than any thing I've ever driven!  Even on winding dirt roads it sticks like glue.  I can get the rear end to brake loose with throttle but as yet it hasn't scared me once.  The only time it pushes is if I accelerate hard from a dead stop with the wheel turned.  I can get the front end off the ground for a few feet if I hold the brakes and run it up.  It weighs 1700 pounds with me on it.  I'd be happy to help anyone interested.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero mistress||05-24-2000||10:12 AM||||Your Fieroha looks great!  I bet it turns heads better than ANY Fiero.<P>I would like to know...do you have to have a motorcycle drivers license to drive it?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||triker||05-24-2000||11:33 AM||||My trike is registered as a motorcycle and requires a motorcyle license though I don't have one.  The guy at DMV (who was very helpful in the whole process) said not to worry about it since the cops wouldn't know what was required.  I do get lots of looks, but it's little boys from about 5 up into the early teens that I get a kick out of.  A lot of them actually squeal when I go by.||||reg||1
